finding: P2 src/standard/rendering/engine.ts:181: Delegating lit to litPbr changes point/spot response within 0.01 world units of the light. The removed pointFactor used L = toLight * inverseSqrt(max(distSq, 0.0001)); litPbr (lines 143-144) uses L = toLight / sqrt(max(distSq, 1e-8)). For toLight=(0,0,0.005), normal=(0,0,1), old diffuse cosine is 0.5 and new is 1, doubling a point light's diffuse contribution with the same finite radius attenuation. Spot cone attenuation changes too because spotFactor consumes L. This is a valid finite configuration (e.g. positive source radius), independent of shadow visibility or NotShadowCaster, and is not covered by the reported voxel/gallery captures. The no-consumer-response-change claim therefore fails. Independently examined: commit and parent engine implementation, shade BRDF/BRDF sphere, distanceAttenuation/spotFactor, public lit exports and voxel/materials call sites, and retained voxel profiling oracle. Scoped search found no remaining lightFactor/pointFactor readers. No edits or hardware measurements made. Notes: JSDoc use guidance is useful; ordinary finite BRDF algebra eliminates specular at the supplied material inputs, but this does not establish identical light-direction handling or bitwise f32 accumulation. Interleaved hardware ratios are useful scene-specific cost evidence; overlapping ranges alone do not prove equivalence or exclude a small regression (reported medians rise ~2.9%). Other hardware/custom point-heavy workloads remain unmeasured.
